  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter the last day of dry weather for the area a cold front is \nthen forecast for Friday although a clean frontal passage will \nnot be until toward the end of the weekend. The cold front is \nforecast to sag south into the area starting Friday morning and\nthen will proceed to move southward throughout the day. This \nfeature will provide high chances for widespread shower activity\nwith the possibility of a few storms which should be isolated \nin nature although worse case scenario would be some scattered \nthunderstorm activity through the afternoon. \n\nWith the lack of modest shear and instability the severe \nthunderstorm threat will be limited so just the general run of \nthe mill storms are possible and a few could become strong. With\nsufficient DCAPE and PWAT values the main threat will be \nlocalized flash flooding. This would be possible across low \nlying or flood prone areas if hit repeatedly by heavy shower or\nthunderstorm activity. WPC does have us in a marginal threat \nfor excessive rainfall for much of the area, however since we \nhave been dry and flash flood guidance will be high the risk \nwould be just across those aforementioned areas that are \nbasically flood prone or known nuisance areas. \n\nThe cold front will continue to move southward and out of the \narea but meander to our south for Saturday. This will keep \nchances for shower or even a few thunderstorms across the \nsouthern portions of our CWA across southern West Virginia and \nsouthwest Virginia. The severe threat will again be limited so \njust thinking general thunderstorm activity along with the \npossibility of localized flash flooding. WPC has been \nadvertising a marginal risk for excessive rainfall for Saturday \ninto Sunday in association with this expected activity. Isolated\nflash flooding is possible under heavy downpours which could \nlead to issues across low lying or flood prone areas, especially\nin know nuisance areas who are hit repeatedly. \n\nThe meandering semi-stationary frontal boundary will then have \na wave of low pressure ride along the boundary line and up into\nour part of the area, however should stay in the southern \nportions. This feature will provide additional chances even \nfurther to the north for Sunday. We cannot rule out \nthunderstorm chances close to the low center in portions of our\nsouthernmost areas in the CWA, especially diurnally driven. \nHowever, temperatures by Saturday will be below seasonable for \nmuch of the area, especially north of the frontal boundary. \n\nThis semi-stationary frontal boundary will then be pushed out \nby late Sunday and early Monday as another reinforcing cold \nfrontal boundary to the north is brought in by a parent low \npressure clipper type system. This frontal boundary will be \nmoisture starved and provided little to no additional shower or\nstorm activity. This feature will then be followed by strong \nhigh pressure building in which will provide the region with \nmuch drier and maintain the cooler weather for the new work \nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWith the main weather maker exiting toward the south by Monday,\nstrong high pressure builds in from the north and advects in \ncold air which will maintain temperatures down below seasonable\nfor the new work week. This high pressure system will provide a\nbreak from unsettled weather for the rest of the work week and \npossibly beyond.",
